    "create_time": 1779680430.071,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "All three work; quick guide:\n\n- **Edison:** best for picking the *phrase* first. Load the sample, find the 2–8 bar section you like, trim, normalize, then drag it into Slicex.\n- **Slicex:** best for chopping. It auto-detects transients (adjust via \"Detect beats\" sensitivity) or you place markers manually. Each slice maps to a key, so you can replay chops on the piano roll in a new order. Has per-slice pitch/time settings.\n- **Fruity Slicer:** older, simpler, tempo-syncs slices to the beat automatically. Fine for drum loops, less flexible for melodic chops.\n\nWorkflow: Edison → choose phrase → Slicex → manual markers on musical phrases (not every transient) → play chops on a pad controller/piano roll in a new pattern → swing the drums 55–60% for boom bap.\n\nFor a dusty feel: pitch the sample down 1–3 semitones (Slicex speed/pitch), low-pass around 8–10 kHz, add a little vinyl noise."
     ]
    },
