  "4c9ae0b1-7490-4f43-a91d-8823b388fad0": {
   "id": "4c9ae0b1-7490-4f43-a91d-8823b388fad0",
   "message": {
    "id": "4c9ae0b1-7490-4f43-a91d-8823b388fad0",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1779680545.007,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Legally, yes. Uploading to SoundCloud is still distributing a copyrighted recording and composition without permission, even if you don't make money. In practice:\n- SoundCloud and YouTube run content ID; uncleared samples can get tracks taken down, muted, or monetization claimed by the rightsholder.\n- Small uploads often go unnoticed, but that's risk tolerance, not legality.\n- Safer routes: royalty-free sample packs (read the license; most allow commercial use of beats but not the raw samples), replaying the part yourself (interpolation still needs composition clearance), or heavily transformed chops (still not legally \"safe\").\n\nI'm not a lawyer. If you ever want to release something commercially, clearance or a licensed pack is the way."
     ]
    },
